  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nTonight and Thursday\n\nThe 12.12z models are in general agreement that a shortwave trough, \ncurrently located over eastern Idaho and central Montana, will move \neast across South Dakota and Nebraska this afternoon and evening. As \nthis occurs, scattered showers and storms will develop. With 0-6 km \nshear over 40 knots, some of these storms will become supercellular. \nAs the evening progresses, the cold pools will coalesce and a linear \nmesoscale convective complex will develop on the nose of a \n30-40 knot low-level jet. Wind gusts could be in the 60 to 80 \nmph. At this time, it looks like these winds would be southwest \nand south of the area overnight and southeast of the area on \nThursday morning.\n\nLow temperatures will be in the lower and mid-60s tonight. High \ntemperatures will range from the mid-70s to mid-80s. The cooler \ntemperatures will be along and south of Interstate 90 where there \nwill be a better chance of showers and storms from late tonight into \nThursday.\n\nThursday night into Saturday\n\n2 to 3 shortwave troughs will move along the northern periphery of a \n500 mb ridge centered over the western Gulf. In addition, a \nstationary front will linger across northern Iowa, southern \nWisconsin, and northern Illinois. Surface-based CAPES will range \nfrom 2000 to 3500 J/kg south of this front. With precipitable water \nvalues will range from 1.5 to 2.25 inches along and south of \nInterstate 90 and warm-cloud layer depths around 4 km, heavy rain \nwill be possible. From Thursday night into Friday south of \nInterstate 90, there is up to a 30 percent chance for 1 inch or \ngreater and up to a 15 percent chance for 2 inches or greater. With \nweak 0-3 km and 0-6 km shear, not anticipating any organized \nsevere weather.\n\nHigh temperatures on Friday and Saturday will range from the mid-70s \nto lower 80s. Low temperatures on Thursday night and Friday night \nwill range from the mid-50s to mid-60s.\n\nSaturday night into Sunday night\n\nThe models are continuing to trend more and more south with the \nfrontal boundary. As a result, the rain chances were reduced some. \nIf this trend continues, we may have to eventually remove the rain \nchances.\n\nHigh temperatures on Sunday will range from the mid-70s to lower \n80s. Low temperatures will be in the lower and mid-60s on Saturday \nnight and range from the lower 50s to lower 60s on Sunday night.",
